        "original_id": "23421d8c-cbd0-5170-a42a-71f43ef10276",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Istanbul, Turkey, Ayakapı, Sultanahmet Square, Fatih district\n- Buildings: Gül Mosque, Hippodrome of Constantinople\n- Organizations: Turkish and Islamic Arts Museum\n\nFacts:\n- Gül Mosque is in Istanbul, Turkey, and its Turkish name Gül Camii means \"The Mosque of the Rose\" in English.\n- Gül Mosque is a former Eastern Orthodox church that the Ottomans converted into a mosque.\n- Ayakapı contains Gül Mosque, which is one of the most important surviving Byzantine buildings on Istanbul's historical peninsula.\n- The Hippodrome of Constantinople is today a square in Istanbul named Sultanahmet Meydanı, or Sultan Ahmet Square, and only a few fragments of the original structure survive.\n- The Turkish and Islamic Arts Museum is a museum in Sultanahmet Square in the Fatih district of Istanbul, Turkey.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "9861c7cd-899d-51a5-8284-0a804310deb2",
        "text": "Page: Gül Mosque. Sentence: Gül Mosque (Turkish: \"Gül Camii\" , meaning: \"The Mosque of the Rose\" in English) is a former Eastern Orthodox church in Istanbul, Turkey, converted into a mosque by the Ottomans.\nPage: Hippodrome of Constantinople. Sentence: Today it is a square named \"Sultanahmet Meydanı\" (\"Sultan Ahmet Square\") in the Turkish city of Istanbul, with a few fragments of the original structure surviving.\nPage: Ayakapı. Sentence: In Ayakapı lies one of the most important surviving Byzantine buildings of the historical peninsula, the Gül Mosque.\nPage: Turkish and Islamic Arts Museum. Sentence: The Turkish and Islamic Arts Museum (Turkish: \"\" ) is a museum located in Sultanahmet Square in Fatih district of Istanbul, Turkey.",
